# Frogs sound board -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# Sega/Gremlin G-80 games carry a sound board of their own, driven from the CPU board's
# output ports: the port bits arrive here as ports (MAME's TTL_INPUT names, HI_D0 ...
# LO_D7 or SOUND_0 ...), and the mixed sound goes out to the amplifier.
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/sega/nl_frogs.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(frogs). Licence CC0-1.0 by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders Aaron Giles (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/sega/nl_frogs.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 24: #define ENABLE_FRONTIERS 1 -> 0 (solver partitioning only)
# line 25: #define UNDERCLOCK_NOISE_GEN 1 -> 0 (a part run slower than the board runs it)
# line 370: RU4 is not a part -- a 100K to ground on a 555 output the board leaves unconnected -- MAME's file says the netlist 'doesn't like unconnected 555 outputs'
# line 380: RU15 is not a part -- a 100K to ground on a 555 output the board leaves unconnected -- MAME's file says the netlist 'doesn't like unconnected 555 outputs'
# Stand-ins that are not parts (the nets they drive are ports): TTL_INPUT(I_SOUND_0), TTL_INPUT(I_SOUND_1), TTL_INPUT(I_SOUND_2), TTL_INPUT(I_SOUND_3), TTL_INPUT(I_SOUND_4), TTL_INPUT(I_SOUND_5), TTL_INPUT(I_SOUND_6), TTL_INPUT(I_SOUND_7)
#
# Left out by MAME (commented out at TAG, so not in this file either):
#   line 138: RES(R60, RES_K(10))     -- final amp
#   line 175: CAP(C1, CAP_U(10))
#   line 176: CAP(C2, CAP_U(10))
#   line 208: CAP(C34, CAP_U(0.05))   -- final amp
#   line 209: CAP(C35, CAP_U(0.05))   -- final amp
#   line 232: CAP(C58, CAP_U(0.1))    -- final amp
#   line 297: LM741_DIP(U13)          // Op. Amp. -- final amp
#
# Not compared with the sheet. MAME's designators, values and pin numbers are taken as
# MAME has them; the checker shows the netlist is internally consistent (every pin exists,
# no two outputs fight, nothing floats), which is not the same as matching the board.

